relations.\n\nThe descriptions emphasize his critique of morality, religion, and the foundations of society, as well as his interpretation of culture and existence. They portray his philosophical method as dialectical and highlight his engagement with themes such as difference, repetition, memory, and representation, alongside critiques of affirmation and negation. Some descriptions attribute to him the idea that affirmation is primary and that difference is affirmed within the logic of being and negation. The eternal return (eternal recurrence) is repeatedly linked to his thought, though the text notes that in some passages Nietzsche discusses the concept without providing a full, explicit exposition; nevertheless, the idea remains central to his project.\n\nIn sum, Nietzsche is presented as a foundational influence on how the text explores history, identity, and social production—through a synthesis of critiques of morality and religion, analyses of culture and power, and the enduring philosophical motifs of the will to power, metamorphosis, the eternal return, difference, and affirmation.", "VIRILIO:VIRILIO refers to Paul Virilio, a philosopher and theorist noted for his rigorous analysis of the depopulation of the people and the deterritorialization of the earth. In the referenced text, VIRILIO is described as asserting that war arises from the relation of hunting and animal training, and as discussing the concepts of the war machine and speed.", "MALLARME:MALLARME refers to Stéphane Mallarmé, a prominent French symbolist poet of the 19th century. He is celebrated for his intricate use of language and dense symbolism, qualities that have made his work a central touchstone in discussions of French symbolist poetry. In various passages, Mallarmé is invoked as evoking religious fear and repentance, underscoring the spiritual and existential tensions often read into his poetry. He is also associated with the claim that the Book needed a people, highlighting a belief in literature’s necessity to speak to and from a readership. Additionally, Mallarmé’s name appears in references to his Book as an example of problematic works, reflecting critical debates about interpretation, accessibility, and reception. Collectively, these descriptions position Stéphane Mallarmé as a key figure whose complex linguistic artistry, symbolic depth, and provocative ideas about literature's relationship to readers continue to provoke thoughtful analysis.", "KAFKA:KAFKA refers to Franz Kafka, the renowned writer. In the provided descriptions, Franz Kafka is cited in a text with the quote “the sneer of its capital error,” a phrase associated with death and existential meaning. He is also described as a writer cited for the idea that literature is the affair of the people, indicating an understanding of literature as something intrinsically connected to and concerned with the concerns of the broader public.", "MARSHALL MCLUHAN:Marshall McLuhan, a prominent Canadian media theorist, is cited in the passage as describing neoprimitivism and a new tribal society.)<|COMPLETE|>", "PLATO:PLATO, ancient Greek philosopher (c. 428–348 BCE), foundational figure in Western philosophy, student of Socrates, and founder of the Academy. He is repeatedly cited as the author of dialogues such as The Republic, Philebus, Phaedo, and Timaeus, through which his enduring influence on ethics, metaphysics, epistemology, and political theory is traced. Central to the described passages is his theory of Forms (Ideas) and his exploration of how the mind apprehends eternal archetypes through reminiscence (anamnesis), linking memory to time and to empirical experience. \n\nIn these texts, Plato is shown as shaping the inquiry into representation, the distinction between the sensible and the intelligible, and the limits of perception and thought. His discussions of the One and the Many—unity versus multiplicity—are invoked to critique abstract, dialectical syntheses and to illuminate the dynamics of difference and repetition in philosophical problems. The notion of division—Plato’s method of dividing a composite into parts, distinguishing genus and species—is presented as an historical precursor to later approaches (notably Bergson’s) that emphasize intuition, problem-solving, and the analysis of difference. \n\nPlato’s dialogues are also used, in Bergson’s readings, to illustrate the gathering and ordering of experience: the idea that a being can provoke a cognitive shock, awaken memory, and constrain thought; and the description of the “contrary-sensible,” a term associated with the sensory realm and its relation to intensity, quality, and sensibility. In some passages, Plato is metaphorically invoked as a demiurge—a creator-like figure who orders differences within the discussion of good sense and time—reflecting the synthetic act of organizing experience rather than a literal cosmological role. \n\nMoreover, Plato’s influence is tied to discussions of reminiscence and the notion that learning is a form of transcendental movement of the soul, with memory serving as the link between the eternal Forms and the changing world. The text repeatedly references Plato as the central figure of Platonism, the proponent of the theory of Forms, and a major source for concepts of representation, division, and the relationship between ideas and sensible extensity. In sum, Plato is portrayed as a seminal thinker whose dialogues established key tools—reminiscence, Forms, division, and the inquiry into unity and multiplicity—that continue to shape philosophical method and the understanding of time, memory, and perception.", "EARTH:Earth is the planet referenced in the text as undergoing changes in meaning under contrasting models (the legal model vs ambulant model) and used to illustrate deterritorialization and reterritorialization within nomadology.)<|COMPLETE|><|COMPLETE|>", "JACQUES RIVIERE:French literary critic mentioned as the recipient of Artaud's letters; the text discusses their correspondence regarding theories of thought, exteriority, and interiority", "KLEIST:German writer referenced for his essay Uber die allmachliche Verfertigung der Gedanken beim Reden, which critiques central interiority of the concept and explores the Gemut (emotional mind) in the formation of ideas", "HUBAC:Hubac is cited in the passage as a commentator who contends that nomadism is explainable more by the divagation of local climates than by universal climate changes, reflecting the text’s discussion on deterritorialization.)<|COMPLETE|>"], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_35abdf9bb8693f3386787f35441ac58912262d10de785c6dd6a86e86f1bdd604_v2"}